Second time around, this go:
In regular dadi/moments workflows, so not pool seq, each site fives a genotype call for each N diploid individual. So allele count at that site is k out of 2N chroms observed. SFS is a histogram 
of those counts. We project down to a smaller fixed sample size to account for missing genotypes at some sites so all sites are comparable.  

Pool seq breaks this convention because we don't have individual genotypes. Instead we have read counts (ad1, ad2) from a pool, but the reads aren't chroms (because it's a pool). 
Extra layer of sampling with this method: sequencing reads WITH REPLACEMENT, from the true AF in the pool. Pool itself is a fixed, finite set of 2N chroms. So one one random fragment of DNA could be read
over and over again (because there are many copies in the pool). So even if the true pool frequency is a fixed 20/96, you could still get a random 1, 3, or 0 alt read for any given site. 

Allele depth vs Allele Frequency 

AF: single point estimate per site. For example: ad2/(ad1+ad2). This gets rid of information about how much read support the estimate had, or its statistical confidence. This only gives us a point 
estimate. 

AD: retains raw counts and sampling variance. Allows hypergeometric projection. Basically asking: if a drew a smaller ssamplle of chroms from a specific site, what would the distribution of allele
counts look like given what I actually observed? To do so, the projection math needs the size of the pool I'm sampling from, not number of reads I'm sequencing. 

